WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.120
Hi! This is Presh Talwalkar

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.040
In this video, I'm going to show you one of my favorite proofs of the 'Pythagorean Theorem'.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.220
We'll start out with a right triangle that has legs of  'A' and 'B', and a hypotenuse 'C'.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.320
The first step is to construct two identical squares...

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.380
... that each have a side length of 'A+B'.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:29.360
Inside each of these squares, we're going to strategically place four copies of the original right triangle.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.360
What remains in each of the figures...

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.100
... is green areas that are squares.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.140
For the square on the left...

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.120
... the lower left green area, has an area of A²...

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.920
... and the upper right square has an area of B².

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.360
For the figure on the right...

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.240
The green square has an area of C².

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.000
Now comes the insight.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.400
We started out with two identical squares...

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:00.880
... in which we placed...

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.640
... four copies of the same triangle.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.780
So that means,

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.840
since the blue areas are equal,

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.660
and the original squares were equal...

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.920
Therefore, the remaining green areas must be equal.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.920
And so what we have...

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.120
Is 'A² + B²' on the left...

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.720
... And C² on the right.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.780
And therefore, 'A² +B² = C²'.

